          "type": "turn",
          "question": "my poo has been really dark, almost black and sticky, for three days and I feel light headed when i stand",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (999, 911, or your local emergency number).** Do not wait for a routine appointment.\n\nStools that are black, tarry and sticky can be a sign of bleeding higher up in the digestive tract. The emergency guidance for gastrointestinal bleeding lists black, tarry, foul-smelling stool as a reason to call emergency services immediately. It also says that any bleeding with dizziness or faintness needs emergency care, \"regardless of how much blood has been seen\" (*Gastrointestinal bleeding*, gastroenterology and emergency medicine guidance). Feeling light-headed when you stand is a sign of low blood pressure, which the guidance treats as a warning sign of significant blood loss.\n\n**What to do right away:**\n- Call emergency services. Tell them you have had black, sticky stools for three days and feel light-headed on standing.\n- Do not drive yourself. Lie down with your legs raised while you wait, unless lying down makes you short of breath.\n- Do not eat or drink anything until a clinician has seen you.\n- Call them back straight away, and do not wait for things to get worse, if you faint, become confused, feel cold and clammy, have chest pain or breathlessness, vomit blood or material that looks like coffee grounds, or pass fresh blood.\n- Tell the team if you take blood thinners (anticoagulants) or have liver disease, as this changes how urgently you are assessed.\n\n**A note on the triage result:** The fixed rule table matched no emergency pattern for this description, so it returned a routine tier. That table does not cover every presentation. The gastrointestinal bleeding guidance matches your symptoms closely, so I have given you the higher tier. Please treat this as an emergency.\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. Only a clinician who examines you can find the cause.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
